; =====================================================================
; envelopes.derived.cddl
;
; DERIVED - not verbatim. RFC 9277 DEFINES THREE ENVELOPES IN PROSE AND
; SUPPLIES CDDL FOR NONE OF THEM. Its four CDDL snippets
; (cddl-snippets.cddl, verbatim) are content-format tag assertions:
; Appendix B.1's two carry only the INNER tag, and Appendix D.1's two
; carry the 55801 header. There is no rule anywhere in the document for
; the CBOR Tag Wrapped envelope or for the Labeled CBOR Sequence label.
; The rules below were written for this record from the prose of
; sections 2.2, 2.3 and Appendix D and MUST NEVER be quoted as the
; source's own grammar.
;
; Derived from RFC 9277:
;   section 2.2      the two nested tags, outer 55799  (rfc-9277#R-0046
;                    to #R-0050)
;   section 2.3      the 12-byte label, outer 55800, inner tag content
;                    'BOR'                 (#R-0061 to #R-0077)
;   Appendix D       the 12-byte header, outer 55801   (#R-0183 to #R-0191)
;   section 2.1      the inner tag value is in
;                    0x01000000..0xFFFFFFFF            (#R-0039)
;   Appendix B       TN(ct) as printed      (#R-0154), and the
;                    "Exceptionally" rule   (#R-0159 to #R-0165)
;
; [correction] 2026-10-20, pass 2. ALL FIVE RANGES ABOVE WERE WRONG, and
; every endpoint resolved to a real but different statement, so nothing
; in bin/ could see it. What they used to say, and what each endpoint
; actually is:
;   section 2.2 was given as R-0045..R-0049. R-0045 is a SECTION 2.1
;     statement ("For CBOR data items that form a representation that is
;     already described by such a Content-Format Number, a tag number
;     has proactively been allocated in Section 4.3...") and the range
;     stopped one short of R-0050, the innermost tag content.
;   section 2.3 was given as R-0059..R-0074. R-0059 and R-0060 are
;     SECTION 2.2.1's fingerprint halves, not section 2.3; and the range
;     stopped at R-0074, three short of section 2.3's last entry.
;   Appendix D was given as R-0168..R-0176. That is APPENDIX B.1 and the
;     first entry of Appendix C -- off by fifteen.
;   section 2.1's tag-range statement was given as R-0040, which is the
;     EMBEDDED-ZERO-BYTE suggestion. The range statement is R-0039.
;   Appendix B was given as R-0133 and R-0138..R-0144. R-0133 is
;     Appendix A's "Some considerations in the form of survey questions
;     follow." and R-0138..R-0144 is APPENDIX A.2's concatenation
;     discussion. The TN formula is R-0154 and the "Exceptionally" rule
;     is R-0159..R-0165.
; The cause is the one pass 1 diagnosed in its own messages.yaml draft:
; a 195-entry id layout written from memory against a 61-entry record's
; shape. It recurred here because pass 1 audited messages.yaml and not
; schema/.
;
; THE DIALECT is CDDL as of RFC 8610, plus RFC 9682's corrected
; collected ABNF. The `#6.<n>(...)` tag notation is RFC 8610 section
; 2.2.2's and is the notation the document's own snippets use.
;
; WHAT CANNOT BE WRITTEN HERE, and it is the reason this file is not a
; complete schema: CDDL CANNOT STATE A TOP-LEVEL CBOR SEQUENCE. That is
; RFC 8742 section 4.1 in its own words -- "Currently, CDDL does not
; provide for unadorned CBOR Sequences as a top-level subject of a
; specification" -- so E2 below is an ARRAY RULE standing in for a
; sequence rule, corrected by a comment, exactly as RFC 8742 section
; 4.1 instructs and exactly as
; records/ietf/rfc-8742/distilled/schema/cbor-seq-container.derived.cddl
; C1 does. The two records hit the same wall from opposite sides.
;
; Source of every quoted sentence: RFC 9277, cached source sha256
;   bd637eafad9ef940c8f5d029076f70c58af7bde6beb38efee5ba7af3690289f7
; =====================================================================

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; E1. CBOR TAG WRAPPED (section 2.2)
;     "This data item is enveloped into two nested tags:"
;     The whole file is ONE CBOR data item.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

cbor-tag-wrapped = #6.55799(protocol-tagged-item)

protocol-tagged-item = #6.<protocol-tag>(any)
  ; CDDL CANNOT WRITE THIS LINE. The inner tag number is a parameter of
  ; the protocol, not a constant, and CDDL's `#6.<n>` requires a literal
  ; `n`: RFC 8610 section 2.2.2 gives `#6.<uint>(type)` with uint a
  ; LITERAL, and no control operator in RFC 8610, RFC 9165, RFC 9741 or
  ; RFC 9682 ranges over tag numbers. (RFC 9682 section 3.2 adds
  ; `#7.<type>` for simple values, which is the nearest thing and does
  ; not reach major type 6.) So a conforming CDDL rule has to name ONE
  ; tag number; a rule for "any registered CBOR Protocol" is not
  ; writable. The pseudo-production above is OURS and is not CDDL.
  ;
  ; The constraint the document does state on that number
  ; (rfc-9277#R-0039 - [correction] 2026-10-21, pass 3: this read
  ; rfc-9277#R-0040, which is the EMBEDDED-ZERO-BYTE suggestion, "It is
  ; further suggested to avoid values that have an embedded zero byte in
  ; the 4 bytes of their binary representation (such as 0x12003456), as
  ; these may confuse implementations that treat the magic number as a C
  ; string." Pass 2 corrected the SAME wrong id in this file's `Derived
  ; from` header above and did not look at the body): "In order to be in the 4-byte range, and so that
  ; there are no leading zero bytes in the 4-byte encoding of the tag
  ; number, the value needs to be in the range 0x01000000 (decimal
  ; 16777216) to 0xFFFFFFFF (decimal 4294967295) inclusive."

; Concrete instance, from section 2.2.1 with TN(112):
senml-tag-wrapped = #6.55799(#6.1668546929([* senml-record]))
senml-record      = any   ; RFC 8428 is not held in this library
  ; NOTE THE TAG CONTENT IS AN ARRAY, NOT A BYTE STRING. That follows
  ; Appendix B's exception -- "Exceptionally, when used immediately as
  ; tag content of one of the tags 55799, 55800, or 55801 ... 1. The
  ; CBOR data item within the representation (without byte-" /
  ; "string wrapping)." -- and it is why Appendix B.1's own
  ; `senml-cbor = #6.1668546929(bstr)` DOES NOT MATCH section 2.2.1's
  ; example. Reported in README.md, not repaired.

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; E2. LABELED CBOR SEQUENCE (section 2.3)
;     "This method prepends a newly constructed, separate data item to
;      the CBOR Sequence, the _label_."
;     The label is a SEPARATE DATA ITEM. The file is a CBOR Sequence.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

; This defines an array, the elements of which are to be used
; in a CBOR Sequence:
labeled-cbor-sequence = [cbor-sequence-label, * any]
  ; THE TOP-LEVEL RULE IS A FICTION and the comment above is the actual
  ; specification of the container, per RFC 8742 section 4.1. A
  ; conforming instance of this rule is a CBOR array; the file is not
  ; one.

cbor-sequence-label = #6.55800(#6.<protocol-tag>('BOR'))
  ; Same unwritable inner tag as E1. Concretely, with TN(272):

missing-blocks-label = #6.55800(#6.1668547090('BOR'))
  ; "The total length of the label is 12 bytes." (rfc-9277#R-0064
  ; - [correction] 2026-10-21, pass 3: this read rfc-9277#R-0062, which
  ; is section 2.3's method sentence, "This method prepends a newly
  ; constructed, separate data item to the CBOR Sequence, the _label_."
  ; - a real statement, and not the one quoted)
  ; Bytes: d9 d9f8 | da 63740212 | 43 | 42 4f 52.
  ; THE 12 BYTES HOLD ONLY UNDER PREFERRED SERIALIZATION, which nothing
  ; in RFC 9277 requires. 30 definite-length spellings are enumerated in
  ; prefix-spellings.derived.txt and exactly ONE is 12 bytes long.
  ; CDDL CANNOT EXPRESS THE DIFFERENCE: a schema constrains the data
  ; model, not the serialisation, so all 30 satisfy this rule. That is
  ; rfc-8949's finding about determinism being an encoder-side property,
  ; and it applies to the one thing this document delivers.

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; E3. CBOR-LABELED NON-CBOR DATA (Appendix D)
;     The ONE envelope the document gives CDDL for, in D.1 - and only
;     for the header, because the payload is not CBOR.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

cbor-labeled-non-cbor-header = #6.55801(#6.<protocol-tag>('BOR'))
  ; Appendix D.1's own two instances, verbatim in cddl-snippets.cddl:
  ;   td-json-header      = #6.55801(#6.1668547250('BOR'))
  ;   json-deflate-header = #6.55801(#6.1668557910('BOR'))
  ; THERE IS NO RULE FOR THE FILE, and there cannot be: "The actual
  ; Non-CBOR Protocol data then follow directly appended to the CBOR
  ; representation of the header." (rfc-9277#R-0189
  ; - [correction] 2026-10-21, pass 3: this read rfc-9277#R-0174, AND
  ; R-0174 IS ONE OF THE TWO DUPLICATE-TEXT IDS bin/check-id-joins
  ; --surface names for this record. It is Appendix B.1's "The following
  ; CDDL snippet can be used to identify application/", byte-identical to
  ; R-0169. The sentence quoted here is Appendix D's and is R-0189. Pass 2
  ; found and fixed the same id in this file's `Derived from` header and
  ; audited no citation in its body) After byte 12 the
  ; file is not CBOR at all, so no CDDL rule can describe it. This is
  ; the only one of the three envelopes whose incompleteness is forced
  ; rather than accidental.

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; E4. THE CONTENT-FORMAT TAG, OUTSIDE AN ENVELOPE (Appendix B)
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

content-format-tagged = #6.<TN-of-ct>(bstr)
  ; The general case, which Appendix B.1 justifies: "Note that a byte
  ; string is used as the type of the tag content because a media type
  ; representation in general can be any byte string."
  ; (rfc-9277#R-0170 - [correction] 2026-10-21, pass 3: this read
  ; rfc-9277#R-0154, which is the TN FORMULA itself, "TN(ct) = 0x63740101
  ; + (ct / 255) * 256 + ct % 255." The sentence quoted is Appendix B.1's
  ; justification for `bstr` and is R-0170. Note the direction of this
  ; one: pass 2 INTRODUCED R-0154 into this file's header as the correct
  ; id for the formula, and R-0154 was already wrong here for a different
  ; sentence)
  ;
  ; AND THE TAG CONTENT IS NOT DETERMINED BY THE TAG NUMBER. Immediately
  ; inside 55799 the content is the bare item or the sequence's items in
  ; an array; immediately inside 55800 or 55801 it is 'BOR'; anywhere
  ; else it is a byte string. Section 4.3's registry Data Item field is
  ; a disjunction for this reason -- "byte string or any CBOR data item
  ; (see Appendix B)" -- and a decoder cannot resolve it from the tag
  ; number alone. Deciding which reading applies needs the media type's
  ; own structure and its content coding, neither of which the tag
  ; number carries and neither of which the CoAP Content-Formats
  ; registry states as a field. See content-format-tag.derived.yaml
  ; -> tag_content_is_not_determined_by_the_tag_number.

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT NONE OF THE FOUR SUPPLIES, and it is the condition-(4) finding
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; AN AUTHENTICATED TYPE INDICATOR. Under E1 the two tag heads ENCLOSE
; the payload, so they are outside anything the payload signs; under E2
; and E3 the label or header is a SEPARATE DATA ITEM in front of the
; payload, so it is outside it twice over. There is no rule above in
; which the label could be covered by a signature over the content, and
; no rule the document supplies in which it could be. Section 3 states
; the adjacent problem without drawing the conclusion: "Protective
; applications (that check data) cannot rely on the applications they
; try to protect (that use the data) to make exactly the same decisions
; in recognizing file formats.  (This is an instance of a check versus
; use issue.)" So relabelling a stored artifact -- swapping the inner
; tag -- breaks no signature and can route the same bytes to a
; different parser. The canonical-encoding topic's condition (4) is
; NOT discharged by either method. See ../design-notes.md.
; =====================================================================
